| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 2 | 0 | 2 |
| 1 | 0 | 2 | 2 |
| 2 | 2 | 2 | 4 |
| 3 | 3 | 4 | 7 |
| 4 | 0 | 7 | 7 |
| 5 | 1 | 7 | 8 |

Sorted keys: [0, 0, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 5]. Original indices: [3, 6, 0, 4, 2, 5, 7, 1].
\image(figures/counting_sort_blocks.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The six native bucket columns retain absent keys 1 and 4. Ends are cumulative counts before placement, and intervals identify the exact output blocks.)
\alttext(Six columns show each key's frequency, initial cumulative end cursor and half-open output interval. The zero-frequency columns are shaded grey.)

> Actual end cursors and the stability proof

Initialize each mutable cursor p_b=e_b. Visit input indices i=n−1,...,0 and
assign the item to j=−−p_(a_i−L). The cursor is decremented before the write.
After t items of a particular key have been placed, its cursor equals e_b−t;
those items occupy the final t slots of its block. Since exactly c_b items have
that key, its last assignment reaches s_b and cannot underflow or collide with
another bucket. Every input index is visited exactly once. This proves the
output is a permutation with ascending keys.

For equal-key indices i<j, the reverse scan visits j first and gives it a
larger output position. It visits i later and gives it a smaller position.
Thus the order of equal keys is preserved. Using a forward scan with these
same decrementing end cursors would reverse ties. A different valid design
can scan forwards with incrementing start cursors; stability depends on the
combination of cursor convention and scan direction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/counting_sort_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::counting_sort_trace({2,5,3,0,2,3,0,3},0,5,6,8);
  std::print("| execution step | input index | actual key | end before | actual output slot |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t e=0;e<t.placements.size();++e) {
    const auto& p=t.placements[e];
    if(p.cursor_before!=p.output_index+1 || t.analysis.original_indices[p.output_index]!=p.input_index) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",e+1,p.input_index,p.key,p.cursor_before,p.output_index);
  }
  std::vector<double> keys,frequencies;
  for(std::size_t b=0;b<t.analysis.counts.size();++b) {
    keys.push_back(b);frequencies.push_back(t.analysis.counts[b]);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars(keys,frequencies,"actual frequency",{37,99,235},.75)
      .title("Original input: the native count array")
      .x_label("integer key").y_label("frequency")
      .x_limits(-.6,5.6).y_limits(0,3.5).size(900,540)
      .save_svg("build/counting_sort_counts.svg");
  std::print("\nEqual key 3: input indices 7,5,2 are visited in that order; output slots are 6,5,4.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| execution step | input index | actual key | end before | actual output slot |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 7 | 3 | 7 | 6 |
| 2 | 6 | 0 | 2 | 1 |
| 3 | 5 | 3 | 6 | 5 |
| 4 | 4 | 2 | 4 | 3 |
| 5 | 3 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 6 | 2 | 3 | 5 | 4 |
| 7 | 1 | 5 | 8 | 7 |
| 8 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 2 |

Equal key 3: input indices 7,5,2 are visited in that order; output slots are 6,5,4.
\image(figures/counting_sort_counts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The histogram preserves the original report's six buckets. The accompanying table records all eight actual writes; no second sorting loop reconstructs the trace.)
\alttext(A bar chart has frequencies two, zero, two, three, zero and one at keys zero through five.)

The first write takes the last input 3, changes its cursor 7→6 and writes slot
6. The next 3 changes it 6→5, and the earliest 3 finally goes to slot 4.
Reading the finished key-3 block forwards therefore gives original indices
[2,5,7]. This is an observable stability test because identities remain distinct.

> Named records with genuinely equal sorting keys

Retain the original records Ann(1), Bob(0), Cy(2), Dan(1), Eve(0), Fay(1),
Gil(2), Hal(0). Supply only [1,0,2,1,0,1,2,0] as keys. The returned
output-to-input permutation gathers their names without making arrival index
part of the key. A comparison against std::stable_sort uses key alone too.

Packing key×100+arrival into distinct integers, as the former report did,
would test ordinary numeric sorting but could not establish stability:
an unstable numeric sort could produce the same packed result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/counting_sort_stability.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Ann","Bob","Cy","Dan","Eve","Fay","Gil","Hal"};
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> keys{1,0,2,1,0,1,2,0};
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::counting_sort_analyze(keys);
  std::vector<std::size_t> reference(keys.size());std::iota(reference.begin(),reference.end(),0);
  std::stable_sort(reference.begin(),reference.end(),[&](auto i,auto j){return keys[i]<keys[j];});
  if(a.original_indices!=reference) return 1;
  std::print("| output slot | name | unchanged sorting key | original index |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.45;box.height=1.45;box.font_size=17;
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{220,252,231},{219,234,254},{254,243,199}};
  d.text({5.95,2.1},"Input: arrival order",23);
  for(std::size_t j=0;j<keys.size();++j) {
    box.fill=colors[keys[j]];
    d.node(std::format("in{}",j),{1.7*j,.4},std::format("{} ({})\nindex {}",names[j],keys[j],j),box);
    const auto i=a.original_indices[j];box.fill=colors[keys[i]];
    d.node(std::format("out{}",j),{1.7*j,-2.8},std::format("{} ({})\nindex {}",names[i],keys[i],i),box);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",j,names[i],keys[i],i);
  }
  d.text({5.95,-1.2},"Stable output: indices increase inside each colour group",20)
   .text({5.95,-4.4},"Bob, Eve, Hal  |  Ann, Dan, Fay  |  Cy, Gil",20)
   .save_svg("build/counting_sort_stability.svg");
  std::print("\nUnpacked equal-key identities match std::stable_sort: yes.\n");
}
```
