// result_end
\caption(The two observed curves are k and 256+k for n=128. For k>128 many buckets are empty, yet the explicit range still receives a complete prefix scan.)
\alttext(Two straight curves rise with explicit bucket count. Prefix work equals k, while the selected total remains 256 above it.)

A trillion-wide range is unsuitable even for two input values. The legacy
sorting overloads keep their inclusive hard ceiling of 1,000,000,000 buckets;
this is a compatibility limit, not a practical memory recommendation. Their
two size_t bucket arrays alone would require about 16 GB at that ceiling on
a 64-bit platform. Analysis and tracing default to at most 1,000,000 buckets;
tracing additionally defaults to 100,000 placement events. A zero optional cap
disables that cap only, leaving the hard ceiling and container limits in force.
Returned diagnostics retain three bucket arrays and n identities as well as
sorted keys. Temporary arrays and optional events add further peak storage.
Allocation can still throw std::bad_alloc below any cap.

All bounds and keys are validated before bucket allocation or budget checks.
The mathematical width is checked using unsigned differences, avoiding signed
overflow for extreme bounds and rejecting a full signed-64-bit span safely.
Every accepted nonempty diagnostic call returns complete results; it never
silently truncates events. Inverted ranges fail even for empty input. Valid
empty explicit input retains its bounds but allocates no buckets and skips
span and bucket-budget checks, preserving the legacy no-op convention.

The original deterministic example needs no external dataset: tiny duplicate
keys make each cursor and identity inspectable. Exhaustive sequences and random
oracle checks test sorting and stability independently of the report figures.

> References and verification scope

Pat Morin's [Open Data Structures, Section 11.2](https://opendatastructures.org/ods-cpp/11_2_Counting_Sort_Radix_So.html)
gives a reverse-placement implementation and its stability/radix connection.
The [NIST counting-sort entry](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/countingsort.html)
provides the histogram interpretation. The signed bounds, ownership, complete
budgets and work definitions above are datamunge contracts.

Native tests compare all 29,524 ternary sequences through length nine with
std::stable_sort on original indices, plus 1,000 random duplicate-heavy inputs.
Every actual event is checked on the worked example and random fixtures.
Additional controls cover both signed endpoints, all-equal/empty/singleton
inputs, exact and one-below budgets, disabled optional caps, full invalid-key
validation and unchanged legacy input on failure. Sanitizers check the focused
suite. These establish the reported integer properties; no elapsed-time or
universal speed advantage is inferred from the operation-count plot.
